<capabilities>
<p>The Aerospace Carrier variant replaces the BattleMech bays with facilities for forty aerospace fighters, making it one of the largest dedicated fighter carriers ever fielded by the SLDF. Six small craft bays supplement the fighter wing, providing utility transport, search and rescue, and shuttle capability. With 1,057.5 tons of cargo capacity, the carrier variant can sustain prolonged aerospace operations with ample stores of ammunition, fuel, and replacement parts for its embarked fighter wing.</p><p>The defensive systems, armor, drive, and structural characteristics remain identical to the BattleMech Carrier, with ninety tons of ferro-aluminum armor, 203 double heat sinks, and the same powerful drive system. The Aerospace Carrier was designed to operate as the hub of fighter operations during large-scale fleet engagements, launching and recovering its forty fighters while its own weapons contributed to the defensive screen. The vessel's acceleration advantage over slower transports allowed it to reposition rapidly within a formation to provide fighter cover where needed.</p>
</capabilities>

<overview>
<p>The Lee class DropShip was one of the last major military transports produced for the Star League Defense Force before the fall of the Star League. Entering production in 2756, this massive 17,000-ton Spheroid was conceived as part of an ambitious effort to streamline the SLDF's military transport fleet by fielding a single heavily armed and armored vessel capable of delivering large formations faster than any existing design. At twice the size of the standard Overlord, the Lee represented a new generation of military transport that emphasized speed, protection, and payload capacity in equal measure.</p><p>The Lee was produced in four distinct configurations to serve different roles within SLDF combined-arms doctrine. Its primary design carried a reinforced battalion of BattleMechs supported by aerospace fighters, while alternate configurations served as dedicated fighter carriers, armored vehicle transports, and fast cargo runners. This versatility made the Lee a cornerstone of late Star League military logistics, though the class would prove tragically vulnerable to the automated defense systems that guarded the Terran Hegemony during its darkest hours.</p>
</overview>

<deployment>
<p>The Aerospace Carrier variant served as a mobile fighter base for SLDF fleet operations, deploying its forty-fighter air wing during major engagements. These carriers proved particularly valuable during the Periphery Uprising, where the ability to project aerospace superiority from a single fast platform gave SLDF commanders decisive advantages in orbital combat. During the Liberation of Terra, Aerospace Carriers were among the first vessels committed to battle, launching their fighters against the Caspar drones before the main transport fleet advanced.</p>
</deployment>

<history>
<p>The Lee class DropShip originated from a proposal presented to SLDF Commanding General Aleksandr Kerensky in 2749 by a consortium of three major naval yards in the Terran Hegemony. Kerensky's vision was to reduce the proliferation of military DropShip classes by creating a single design capable of carrying large payloads of SLDF troops into battle faster than anything currently in service. The development process was far from smooth, as political entanglements and boondoggles similar to those that had plagued the earlier Colossus class DropShip threatened to derail the program entirely.</p><p>Ironically, Star Lord Richard Cameron's attempts to block the Lee's production proved counterproductive. His opposition galvanized support among the Star League Council members who were increasingly wary of Cameron's erratic governance, and their collective approval pushed the design into production in 2756. The SLDF quickly ordered all four configurations, though the cargo transport variant saw the most limited production run with only three vessels completed before the program's priorities shifted to combat variants.</p><p>The Lee first saw major combat during the Periphery Uprising, where its ability to rapidly move large troop formations proved invaluable to the SLDF's suppression campaigns. The class's generous payload capacity allowed commanders to concentrate forces quickly at trouble spots, and the Lee's heavy armament meant it could contribute directly to orbital combat rather than relying entirely on escorts. These early engagements validated Kerensky's original vision of a fast, versatile military transport.</p><p>The Amaris Coup and subsequent Liberation of Terra transformed the Lee's role from triumphant to tragic. When the SLDF fought to reclaim the Hegemony, the Space Defense System's Caspar drone fleets proved devastatingly effective against the large, relatively slow transports. The Lee's substantial carrying capacity, once its greatest asset, became a catastrophic liability as each vessel destroyed took an enormous number of troops with it. Losses mounted so rapidly that surviving Lee transports were held in reserve until the final landings on Terra itself, where they deployed in large numbers for the climactic assault.</p><p>Few Lee class vessels survived the Liberation of Terra in operational condition, and those that did joined Kerensky's Exodus Fleet on its journey to the deep Periphery. The remaining ships were subsequently lost during the fierce fighting of the Pentagon Civil War, and no examples of the class are known to have survived into the modern era. The Lee stands as both evidence of late Star League engineering ambition and a cautionary example of how concentrated transport capacity can become a strategic vulnerability.</p>
</history>

